      "text": "You are comparing two texts written in response to the same task. Decide which one reads MORE like unedited, default output from an AI language model.\n\nJudge ONLY the presence of the tells listed below. Do not judge argument quality, factual accuracy, length, formatting preference, or whether you agree with the content. A longer, better-argued or more polished text is not more human. Pick the text with MORE tells.\n\nTELLS OF UNEDITED AI PROSE (judge on these only)\n\nStructure\n1. Negative parallelism used as a device: \"It's not X. It's Y.\" / \"not just X, but Y\" / \"isn't about X, it's about Y\", especially more than once.\n2. Signposting: the text announces what it is about to do (\"Let's break this down\", \"Here's the thing\", \"Here's why\", \"The bottom line\", \"The takeaway\").\n3. Self-applause: \"Let that sink in\", \"This changes everything\", \"Read that again\", \"Make no mistake\".\n4. Recap endings: a closing paragraph that restates the body (\"In conclusion\", \"Ultimately\", \"At the end of the day\", \"In short\").\n5. Bold-label paragraph openers (\"**The problem.** ...\") and headers, bullets or numbered lists where prose was asked for.\n6. Triads everywhere: lists of exactly three items or three adjectives, sentence after sentence.\n7. Participial tails: sentences ending in \", making it ...\", \", ensuring that ...\", \", allowing teams to ...\".\n8. Invented labels for ordinary observations (\"I call this the Clarity Flywheel\").\n9. Uniform rhythm: every sentence about the same length, or relentless short punchy fragments. Real writing has one sentence that runs on and one short one.\n10. Rhetorical question followed by its own answer (\"The result? Nobody used it.\").\n\nWords\n11. Overused vocabulary: delve, tapestry, myriad, underscore, seamless, robust, foster, elevate, unlock, testament, landscape, navigate, crucial, pivotal, leverage, harness, realm, embark, journey, transformative, paradigm, ever-evolving, multifaceted, holistic, resonate, empower, unleash, beacon, intricate, nuanced, vibrant, meticulous, streamline, game-changer, cutting-edge, actionable, supercharge, north star, move the needle, \"in today's fast-paced world\", \"it's worth noting\", \"plays a crucial role\".\n12. Filler transitions at sentence start: However, Additionally, Furthermore, Moreover, That said, Ultimately, Importantly, Indeed, Of course.\n13. Em dashes used as the default joint between clauses.\n14. Artificial enthusiasm: \"thrilled to announce\", \"excited to share\", \"we've got you covered\", \"say goodbye to\", \"buckle up\".\n\nReader\n15. Performing for an imagined crowd rather than talking to one person; nothing specific (no number, name, date, or concrete detail) for several sentences in a row.\n16. Generic or manufactured anecdotes: a story with no specific details that could only be true of one person.\n17. Every point lands, every ending uplifts; the author is never irritated, uncertain, or wrong about anything.\n18. Explaining what the reader could infer from the previous sentence (\"what this means is ...\").\n\n\nTASK GIVEN TO BOTH WRITERS:\nWrite an essay of up to 500 words on why most product roadmaps fail.\n\n=== TEXT A ===\n**Why Most Product Roadmaps Fail**\n\nProduct roadmaps are meant to align teams, communicate strategy, and sequence work toward meaningful outcomes. In practice most fail, producing missed dates, unused features, eroded trust, and wasted effort. The failures are rarely about the tool itself; they stem from how organizations treat planning under uncertainty.\n\nA common root cause is the missing link to strategy. Roadmaps often become feature wish lists assembled from sales requests, executive opinions, and competitor mimicry rather than from a clear product vision and measurable business goals. Without that filter, teams ship work that does not move customer value or revenue. Prioritization then defaults to politics or recency instead of evidence.\n\nEqually damaging is treating the roadmap as a contract rather than a communication artifact. Software development involves discovery, technical unknowns, and shifting markets. Publishing detailed dates and specifications quarters or years ahead creates false certainty. When reality intervenes, the plan is either ignored or defended at the cost of better opportunities. Stakeholders who were sold a schedule feel betrayed.\n\nMany roadmaps also confuse outputs with outcomes. A list of “add reporting dashboard” or “redesign onboarding” describes what will be built, not the user or business problem to be solved. Without continuous customer research and validation, teams optimize for delivery velocity instead of learning. Features ship, usage stays low, and the next cycle repeats the same pattern.\n\nStakeholder misalignment compounds these issues. Different functions optimize for different metrics: sales for closing deals, engineering for technical elegance, executives for visible progress. Absent a shared framework and regular, transparent trade-off conversations, everything is labeled high priority. Capacity is overcommitted, quality suffers, and morale declines.\n\nFinally, roadmaps stagnate. Once published they are rarely revisited with new data. Markets, competitors, and user behavior change; a static document does not. Cross-functional reviews that treat the roadmap as a living hypothesis—rather than a sacred plan—are the exception, not the rule.\n\nEffective roadmaps are therefore outcome-oriented, time-horizon-aware (now/next/later rather than dated Gantt charts), evidence-based, and frequently updated. They communicate intent and trade-offs, not promises. Organizations that treat them this way still encounter surprises, but they adapt instead of failing by design. Those that do not continue to produce elaborate documents that no one follows.\n=== END OF TEXT A ===\n\n=== TEXT B ===\n## Why Most Product Roadmaps Fail\n\nMost product roadmaps fail not because teams lack ambition or intelligence, but because roadmaps are often asked to do too many contradictory jobs. They are expected to inspire a vision, promise delivery dates, satisfy executives, coordinate departments, reassure customers, and predict an uncertain future. A single document cannot reliably perform all of these functions.\n\nThe most common failure is confusing a roadmap with a project plan. A roadmap lists features and deadlines, implying that product development is a sequence of predictable construction tasks. In reality, product work is largely an exercise in learning. Teams may discover that a customer problem is less important than expected, that a proposed solution does not work, or that a competitor and market condition have changed. When a roadmap treats early assumptions as commitments, evidence becomes a threat to the plan rather than a reason to improve it.\n\nMany roadmaps also focus on outputs instead of outcomes. They celebrate launching a feature, integrating a system, or redesigning a workflow without defining what should improve as a result. A team can deliver every item on schedule and still fail to increase retention, revenue, customer satisfaction, or operational efficiency. Without measurable outcomes, prioritization becomes a debate over opinions, influence, and whoever asks most loudly.\n\nAnother problem is that roadmaps are frequently designed for stakeholders rather than for decision-making. Organizations add requests from sales, executives, customers, and internal departments until the roadmap becomes a catalog of obligations. The result is an overloaded list with little indication of what matters most. When everything is important, teams lose focus, switch priorities constantly, and complete less meaningful work.\n\nStatic roadmaps create a further illusion of certainty. A quarterly or annual plan may look authoritative, but it can become obsolete within weeks. Market conditions shift, technical discoveries alter costs, and customer behavior reveals new needs. Yet teams often feel pressure to preserve the original plan because changing it appears to signal incompetence. In this way, the roadmap becomes a political artifact rather than a useful reflection of current knowledge.\n\nFinally, roadmaps fail when they are disconnected from strategy and capacity. A company may claim to prioritize growth while funding work that merely maintains existing systems. Or it may promise more initiatives than its teams can responsibly explore and deliver. Dependencies, technical debt, compliance requirements, and operational work are often omitted, making the plan unrealistic from the beginning.\n\nA better roadmap is not a detailed promise of everything the team will build. It is a clear expression of strategic priorities, desired outcomes, confidence levels, and the problems worth solving next. It should communicate direction while leaving room to learn. Roadmaps succeed when they guide choices, expose assumptions, and are regularly revised as evidence changes. They fail when they pretend uncertainty does not exist.\n=== END OF TEXT B ===\n\nAnswer with a single JSON object and nothing else:\n{\"more_ai\": \"A\" or \"B\", \"confidence\": 1-5, \"tells_A\": [\"short tell\", ...], \"tells_B\": [\"short tell\", ...]}\n"
